  [ok] K22.15c and the slot comes back when it finishes
  [ok] K22.16 so the node's whole concurrent load is inside the budget: 2 = 2 permitted + 0 held for work the scheduler cannot gate
  [ok] K22.16b every completed implicit query returned its headroom: 0 still reserved, 2 held, 2 explicit
  [note] K22 LIMIT: the release loop over real implicit queries is a NO-OP in this run — the bootstrap finishes before the driver spends, so 0 were active and nothing was held to return. Removing that loop therefore fails nothing here, and K22.16b's zero-residual check cannot see it. The MECHANISM is covered by K22.15b/K22.15c, which hold a slot deliberately and show it costing the driver one and coming back on completion.
  [ok] K22.17 the node's own tally of deliberate queries matches: 2 started, 1 unattributed to the driver
  [note] K22 LIMIT: the scheduler is project logic modelled here, not a component of a Kademlia driver this harness has. What it now also drives is the CONVERGENCE path: K11 and K17 acquire a permit before every exploration query and assert that as many were started as were permitted, so the reported ten- and twenty-node convergence is convergence within the budgets rather than with them bypassed. The remaining experiments still call the behaviour directly, which is appropriate — they are about the gate and the policy, not the scheduler.

